<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#JobOffer> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Oferta laboral"@es .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#hasNationality> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/ratr-o#DemographicProfile> .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#TraffickingRoute> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An origin-to-destination pair, optionally with stopover countries, along which victims are moved. Routes in the demonstrator are derived from aggregated citizenship and country-of-exploitation pairs in the CTDC global synthetic dataset, never from individual case records."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#EmotionalConnectionStoryPost> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Relato: conexion emocional (NR*A)"@es .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#schemePalermoPurpose> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "scheme Palermo purpose"@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#CriminalRecruitmentOffer> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:nca1f9ca499024b5c907991352a4826a5b2 .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#exhibitsPurpose> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Relates a job offer to the Palermo Purpose it is believed to serve. Assertion of a purpose is an interpretive step and in the demonstrator it is only made when an evidence source states the exploitation outcome."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#Candidate> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Candidato"@es .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#OrganRemovalRecruitmentScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The offer promises medical treatment, paid egg donation, or a health procedure. The real purpose is extraction of organs (most commonly kidneys) or tissue for sale on the black market. Traffickers promise US$500 to US$10,000 that the victim often never receives. Documented in detail by the UNODC Assessment Toolkit on Trafficking in Persons for the Purpose of Organ Removal (2015, 60+ pages). Explicitly named as a purpose of trafficking by Article 3(a) of the Palermo Protocol (2000). Recruitment uses social media, word of mouth in vulnerable communities, and classifieds."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#tipEventRegisteredBy> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#VisaFraudScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The offer promises employment in a foreign country (commonly Canada, the United States or Western Europe for Latin American victims) and demands payment for visa, gestor or recruiter services. The intermediary disappears after collecting payment. Commonly marketed as domestic work, caregiving, nanny placement or hospitality abroad."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#offeredRole> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "rol ofrecido"@es .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#recruitedVia> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/ratr-o#Victim> .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#FishingVesselLabourScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Offer of work on deep-sea fishing vessels with promised wages and return dates. Victims are taken to international waters where they are held for months or years without pay, with document confiscation, physical abuse and no possibility of exit. Extensively documented by the International Labour Organization ('Caught at Sea', 2013) and by the Environmental Justice Foundation. Principal victim populations include Myanmar, Cambodia, Indonesia and the Philippines; vessels often registered in Thailand, Taiwan, Korea."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#ForeignLanguageTeacherScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/ratr-o#FraudScheme> .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#evidenceFor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "evidencia de"@es .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#offerScreenshotUrl> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/ratr-o#JobOffer> .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#MeansAbuseOfPower> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/ratr-o#PalermoMeans> .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#platformModeratedBy> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/ratr-o#Platform> .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#lexiconSourceLocation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "location in source"@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#SocialMediaPost> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Publicación en red social"@es .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#flagType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "flag type"@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#termDocumentedIn> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Relates a lexicon term to the evidence source that documents it. Every term in the TikTok recruitment lexicon carries this link to the research report and page it was taken from."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#lexiconTermText> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The lexicon term as written in the source."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#lexiconPattern> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/ratr-o#LexiconTerm> .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#PrivateChannelCallSignal> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Llamado al privado"@es .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#FraudRisk> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Riesgo de fraude laboral"@es .
