<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#FraudScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A named operational pattern by which a fraudulent job offer harms the candidate. Independent of the RiskClassification axes: a single offer can exhibit one or more named schemes."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#contactedThrough> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "contacted through"@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#GangRecruitmentSchoolsScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/ratr-o#ChildRecruitmentCriminalScheme> .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o> <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/description> "OWL 2 QL ontology for detecting fraudulent job offers, recruitment fraud and trafficking recruitment in Latin America, designed as the conceptual layer of a Virtual Knowledge Graph."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#GroupEmojiSignal> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/ratr-o#SocialMediaRecruitmentSignal> .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#OnlineCryptoJobScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Advertised as 'crypto trader', 'forex analyst', 'Web3 community manager' or 'blockchain assistant' with high salary, full remote and no experience required. Often used as cover for pay-to-work (candidate buys a 'training course' or deposits 'starting capital'), identity theft (KYC data collected for money-laundering accounts), or cross-border trafficking into Southeast Asian crypto-scam compounds."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#offerClassifierConfidence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/ratr-o#JobOffer> .
