<https://w3id.org/ratr-o> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A domain ontology in OWL 2 QL (DL-Lite) for the detection of fraudulent job offers, labour-recruitment fraud, and human-trafficking recruitment patterns in Latin America. RATR-O models the full continuum of recruitment-based harm: from offers that defraud candidates (identity theft, financial scams, unpaid work) through offers that lead to labour exploitation, through to offers whose purpose is trafficking into forced labour, sexual exploitation, or forced criminality. A single JobOffer can exhibit indicators of any combination of these three outcomes, and RATR-O expresses them as distinct but compatible risk axes. The ontology is designed to operate as the conceptual layer of a Virtual Knowledge Graph over Ramona AI operational data and over open anti-trafficking datasets (CTDC, UNODC), following Xiao, Ding, Cogrel and Calvanese (2019). v1.0 aligns the generalisable fragment of HUTRO (Human Trafficking Ontology, BioPortal, 2025) with Ramona's domain-specific taxonomy of online recruitment fraud in Mexico and Latin America."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#routeLatestYear> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "route latest year"@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#sourceLanguage> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "source language ISO code"@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#CallCenterScamScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The candidate is hired as a legitimate customer-service, collections or sales agent in a call centre that is in fact a fraud operation (time-share extortion, fake investment, virtual kidnapping, romance scams). The worker realises they are defrauding clients and attempts to leave, at which point threats, wage withholding or confiscated documents prevent them from doing so. Documented in Mexico, Costa Rica and the Philippines."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#PurposeSexualExploitation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Explotación sexual"@es .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#VulnerabilityFactor> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Factor de vulnerabilidad"@es .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#PyramidScheme> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The candidate's actual income depends on recruiting further candidates, not on the advertised job. Covers classic pyramid schemes and multi-level-marketing operations disguised as employment."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#routeLatestYear> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Intended datatype xsd:gYear. It is outside the OWL 2 QL datatype map, so the TBox declares rdfs:Literal and the mapping layer emits xsd:gYear literals."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#Recruitment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The ACT of recruitment in the sense of Palermo Protocol Article 3(a). In RATR-O it is the act that a deceptive job offer realises, which is why recruitment-stage fraud is in scope of a trafficking ontology and not only of a consumer-fraud one."@en .
<https://w3id.org/ratr-o#associatedWithRoute> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/ratr-o#JobOffer> .
